          "title": "From Samhain to Supermarkets",
          "page": 1,
          "tasks": [
            {
              "id": "b11",
              "type": "reading-truefalse",
              "title": "The history of Halloween",
              "instruction": "Read and judge the statements.",
              "passage": "Halloween grew out of the ancient Celtic festival of Samhain, which marked the end of the harvest season and the beginning of winter. The Celts believed that on the night of October 31 the boundary between the living and the dead became blurred, so they lit huge bonfires and wore animal skins to confuse restless spirits. When Irish immigrants brought the tradition to America in the 19th century, cheap and plentiful pumpkins replaced turnips as lanterns. 'Trick-or-treating' developed later, partly from the medieval practice of 'guising', where people performed small tricks in exchange for food. Today Halloween is a multi-billion-dollar holiday, yet its central idea remains the same: one night a year when people enjoy being frightened.",
              "statements": [
                {
                  "text": "Samhain celebrated the start of the harvest.",
                  "answer": false
                },
                {
                  "text": "The Celts thought spirits could walk the earth on that night.",
                  "answer": true
                },
                {
                  "text": "Pumpkins were used as lanterns before turnips in Ireland.",
                  "answer": false
                },
                {
                  "text": "Trick-or-treating has links to medieval customs.",
                  "answer": true
                },
                {
                  "text": "Modern Halloween has lost its original idea completely.",
                  "answer": false
                }
              ]
            },
